{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfa5fhXpbg6TA1sFADK1F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the repaired candidate, `glimpse_lapse_ideal_alternation`, and it passes the self-check. The check doesn't test novelty, so whether it clears the near-duplicate gate this time is still unconfirmed.\n\n**Why the last attempt was a duplicate:** it added a shared weight on the longest streak, and that weight fits to about zero. The earlier fits of the same idea put it at 0.04 ± 0.74 and −0.004 ± 0.09, which leaves the incumbent's predictions unchanged. The two sibling slots in this round were rejected for the same reason. The data show why a separate streak weight adds nothing: per participant, avoiding the longer streak correlates at 0.68 with preferring more alternation. So streak aversion goes along with each person's ideal switching rate rather than acting as a separate cue.\n\n**What changed:** the model still refines `personal_lapse_ideal_alternation`, keeping its personal ideal switching rate and personal guessing rate. The one change is where the distance from the ideal is measured. Instead of the whole sequence's alternation rate, each 4-flip window is compared with the ideal, and the squared distances are averaged. That average equals the old overall distance plus a penalty on uneven alternation, such as a long streak next to a stretch that alternates heavily, even when the overall rate is on target. This targets the critique's one discrepancy that survives the FDR correction: among pairs matched on alternation rate, people pick the sequence with the shorter streak more often than the incumbent predicts.\n\n**Why it should pass the novelty gate:** the new penalty has no weight of its own. It is scaled by the incumbent's sensitivity parameter, which fits at about 14, so it can't shrink to zero the way the streak weight did. On pairs where the windows alternate unevenly, I estimate a shift of 